WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.240
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:06.260
A parallel slab shifts a ray sideways but returns it to a parallel direction.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:11.600
A prism has faces that are not parallel, so the ray generally emerges in a

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:12.240
different direction.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.480
We describe that change by its angle of deviation.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.140
Work in the prism's principal section and draw a normal at each face.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.160
The entry angle is i.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.240
The internal angles are r one and r two, each measured from its local normal.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.340
The emergence angle is e.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.900
Geometry gives r one plus r two equal to the prism angle A.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:39.600
The overall deviation equals i plus e minus A for the ordinary transmitted

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.020
path.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.920
Suppose A is sixty degrees, i is forty five, and e is fifty.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.340
The deviation is thirty five degrees.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.060
The incidence and emergence angles are not measured from the prism faces, and

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.720
they do not share one common normal.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.300
That is why a quick sketch is so valuable.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.280
The relation assumes that the ray enters and leaves through the two intended

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:00.660
faces.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:05.080
At some internal angles, total internal reflection can prevent that emerging

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:05.440
path.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.560
Then blindly calculating an emergence angle gives a nonphysical answer.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.200
Our prism drawings are geometry guides, not scale accurate trajectories for

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.160
every choice of index.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.760
Start with the path, label the two normals, and only then use the angle sum.

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:23.548
Rotate an ordinary prism while keeping the incoming ray fixed.

00:01:24.108 --> 00:01:28.088
The deviation first decreases, reaches a minimum, then increases.

00:01:28.728 --> 00:01:33.068
At that minimum for the usual symmetric setup, the internal ray takes a

00:01:33.068 --> 00:01:38.068
symmetric path: i equals e, and r one equals r two.

00:01:38.068 --> 00:01:41.808
Since the internal angles add to A, each is A over two.

00:01:42.848 --> 00:01:44.928
Combine this with Snell's law at the entry face.

00:01:44.928 --> 00:01:49.608
The prism's index relative to its surroundings equals sine of half of A plus

00:01:49.608 --> 00:01:52.888
minimum deviation, divided by sine of half of A.

00:01:53.388 --> 00:01:57.228
Let's use a sixty degree prism with minimum deviation thirty degrees.

00:01:58.008 --> 00:02:02.228
Half of A plus that deviation is forty five degrees; half of A alone is thirty

00:02:02.228 --> 00:02:02.588
degrees.

00:02:02.848 --> 00:02:07.448
The ratio is sine forty five divided by sine thirty, or square root of two.

00:02:07.908 --> 00:02:10.168
This is about one point four one four.

00:02:10.168 --> 00:02:14.568
If the surrounding medium is air approximated as index one, it is also the

00:02:14.568 --> 00:02:16.128
prism's approximate absolute index.

00:02:16.768 --> 00:02:20.948
In another surrounding medium, multiply by that medium's index to obtain the

00:02:20.948 --> 00:02:21.688
absolute prism index.

00:02:22.468 --> 00:02:25.842
Do not use the minimum deviation formula when the given path is not at

00:02:25.842 --> 00:02:26.228
minimum.

00:02:26.728 --> 00:02:29.788
And do not assume i equals e for every orientation.

00:02:30.448 --> 00:02:33.908
The equality is the special symmetry condition we earned by choosing the

00:02:33.908 --> 00:02:34.920
minimum path, not a universal prism rule.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.600
Now reverse the problem.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.540
We know a prism's refractive index and want its minimum deviation.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:45.760
For a sixty degree glass prism in air with index one point five, the internal

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.540
angle at symmetry is thirty degrees.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.920
Snell's law gives sine i equal to one point five times sine thirty, or zero

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.320
point seven five.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.260
The incidence angle is about forty eight point six degrees.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.393
Minimum deviation is twice that angle minus sixty, approximately thirty seven

00:03:02.393 --> 00:03:03.420
point two degrees.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.560
Let's pause for an error check.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.560
Is the index ratio below one?

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.820
No, so the ray bends toward the normal on entering, consistent with an

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.380
incidence angle larger than the thirty degree internal angle.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.680
And is the sine argument physically allowed?

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.100
Zero point seven five is below one.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:19.980
Good.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.975
In general, delta minimum equals twice the inverse sine of relative index

00:03:24.975 --> 00:03:25.280
times

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.700
sine of half the prism angle, minus A.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.188
If that inverse sine argument exceeds one, the usual symmetric transmitted

00:03:32.188 --> 00:03:32.420
path

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:33.180
cannot exist.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.060
A calculator error is telling us something physical, not asking for a creative

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:38.800
replacement number.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.140
Keep degree and radian modes consistent.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.660
These angle relations do not require a small angle approximation.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.300
The thin prism shortcut coming next does, and we should not silently swap the

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:49.780
two formulas.

00:03:50.736 --> 00:03:56.176
For a thin prism, with a small apex angle and sufficiently small incidence and

00:03:56.176 --> 00:04:00.696
refraction angles, Snell's law simplifies because sine theta is approximately

00:04:00.696 --> 00:04:01.756
theta in radians.

00:04:02.476 --> 00:04:07.056
At entry, i is approximately relative index times r one.

00:04:07.056 --> 00:04:11.636
At exit, e is approximately relative index times r two.

00:04:11.636 --> 00:04:15.516
Add the two equations and use r one plus r two equal to A.

00:04:16.236 --> 00:04:21.396
Subtract A, and we get deviation approximately relative index minus one, times

00:04:21.396 --> 00:04:21.616
A.

00:04:22.576 --> 00:04:26.656
So a small four degree prism with index one point five in air gives an

00:04:26.656 --> 00:04:28.476
approximate deviation of two degrees.

00:04:29.056 --> 00:04:33.296
We derived the relation using radians, but the proportional result works with

00:04:33.296 --> 00:04:36.796
degrees too when A and deviation use the same angular unit.

00:04:37.316 --> 00:04:40.716
This shortcut is not for the sixty degree prism from our earlier example.

00:04:41.616 --> 00:04:44.225
Applying it there would give thirty degrees instead of the more accurate

00:04:44.225 --> 00:04:44.443
thirty

00:04:44.443 --> 00:04:45.816
seven point two at minimum.

00:04:46.356 --> 00:04:50.696
The discrepancy is a warning about the approximation, not a contradiction in

00:04:50.696 --> 00:04:51.016
optics.

00:04:51.796 --> 00:04:56.716
If a JEE question explicitly says a thin prism and gives small angles, this

00:04:56.716 --> 00:04:57.896
relation can save time.

00:04:58.636 --> 00:05:01.816
Otherwise use Snell's law and the actual prism geometry.

00:05:02.496 --> 00:05:06.596
The reason a larger index gives more deviation in this ordinary setup is that

00:05:06.596 --> 00:05:09.656
each interface bends the ray more relative to its normal.

00:05:09.936 --> 00:05:13.868
A prism can spread white light into colors because its refractive index

00:05:13.868 --> 00:05:14.256
depends

00:05:14.256 --> 00:05:14.896
on frequency.

00:05:15.536 --> 00:05:20.016
In the ordinary visible normal dispersion region of glass, violet has a larger

00:05:20.016 --> 00:05:20.816
index than red.

00:05:21.816 --> 00:05:25.196
Violet is therefore deviated more in the usual prism arrangement.

00:05:25.876 --> 00:05:28.436
The prism is not changing red light into violet light.

00:05:29.136 --> 00:05:33.296
At stationary interfaces the frequency of each component stays the same.

00:05:33.976 --> 00:05:36.536
It redirects the components by different amounts.

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:42.136
For a thin four degree prism, suppose red has index one point five zero and

00:05:42.136 --> 00:05:44.996
violet has index one point five two.

00:05:44.996 --> 00:05:47.016
Red deviation is approximately two degrees.

00:05:47.916 --> 00:05:50.056
Violet deviation is two point zero eight degrees.

00:05:50.856 --> 00:05:53.796
Their angular separation is zero point zero eight degrees.

00:05:54.516 --> 00:05:57.196
Small, but quite measurable with a suitable arrangement.

00:05:58.356 --> 00:06:02.536
That example also shows why we distinguish deviation of a single color from

00:06:02.536 --> 00:06:04.056
dispersion between two colors.

00:06:04.476 --> 00:06:09.056
If both indices increased equally, both deviations could change without

00:06:09.056 --> 00:06:11.016
changing their separation by the same amount.

00:06:11.796 --> 00:06:16.256
The violet more than red ordering describes ordinary normal dispersion, not

00:06:16.256 --> 00:06:17.896
every material at every frequency.

00:06:18.656 --> 00:06:20.876
Near absorption bands, behavior can differ.

00:06:21.696 --> 00:06:25.236
Finally, remember that total internal reflection and dispersion are different

00:06:25.236 --> 00:06:29.516
effects: one concerns whether a transmitted ray exists, while the other

00:06:29.516 --> 00:06:32.116
concerns how index varies across spectral components.

00:06:32.656 --> 00:06:37.576
Our JEE routine is to identify the regime, label angles, and apply the

00:06:37.576 --> 00:06:39.656
appropriate exact or approximate relation.
